  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 145 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...An unsettled pattern prevails through midweek. \nExpect near average temperatures, with humid conditions, and \ndaily chances of showers and storms.\n\nLatest surface analysis depicted a cold front beginning to push\ninto NW VA early this afternoon. Scattered showers and storms\nare beginning to develop along this front and are expected to\ngradually move SE into this evening with the slow FROPA. An\nadditional area of scattered storms has developed across extreme\nSE VA/NE NC in association with an upper level trough centered\nacross the Southeast. This convection is expected to move NE\nthrough the afternoon before tapering off this evening.\n\n...Previous Discussion...\n\nA shortwave associated with an upper trough will lift across the \narea today, leading to additional convective development today. \nGuidance has trended down some on the coverage of showers and \nthunderstorms, and depict the highest PoPs and QPF across the SE \nportion of the area. The trough weakens further Tuesday and \nWednesday, but scattered afternoon and evening showers and storms \ncapable of heavy rainfall remain expected as deep moisture lingers \ndespite weakening SW flow aloft. The main catalyst for convection \nwill be where the front associated with the slow-moving low across \nthe NE ends up each day, which will ultimately dictate the coverage \nand location of any convective development. The front looks like it \nmay drift across our area on Tuesday, which could bring more shower \nand thunderstorm coverage than previously thought, but will continue \nto monitor any trends in the upcoming forecast cycles. \n\nGiven the environment, and relatively weak unidirectional flow aloft \nand tall/skinny CAPE, there will be a threat of localized flash \nflooding each day. However, the severe threat should stay low due to \npoor mid-level lapse rates, a near-saturated vertical column, and \nweak shear. Days 1-3 (Monday through Wednesday) are in a Marginal \nERO as the overall flash flood risk is more isolated in nature. High \ntemperatures dip down slightly below average during the early week \nperiod thanks to the cloud cover and multiple rounds of \nshowers/tstms, though any breaks in the cloud cover may allow \ntemperatures to briefly overperform.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A more typical summertime pattern returns for \nthe latter half of the week. Isolated to scattered afternoon \nshowers and thunderstorms remain possible, with near to slightly\nabove normal temperatures prevailing through next weekend.\n\nThough not quite as unsettled as the first half of the week, the \nlatter half of the week will see isolated to scattered diurnally \ndriven showers and thunderstorms. With the decreased convective \ncoverage forecast, temperatures will return to near to slightly \nabove normal with highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s. Dew points \nwill remain in the mid to upper 70s, making for quite humid \nconditions. Heat indices will approach 100 degrees for a good \nportion of the area Thursday through Saturday, but due to \ntemperatures remaining near normal, heat headlines are not expected \nat this time, though the dew points will cause it to feel quite\nuncomfortable.",
